      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-4418",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The $4$th row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3 \\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-8)+(0-0) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/example-4418.json",
